WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.919
Speaker 1: Supreme Court Justice Clarence
Thomas, we have now learned, has been for

00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:11.100
years claiming income from a company that
did not actually exist when he said he earned

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.730
income from that company.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:19.260
Now, is this simply a paperwork mistake or
an innocent mixup, or is it the latest in

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:25.880
multiple new chapters of the questions surrounding
Clarence Thomas?

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.510
We are going to discuss all of it.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:31.259
Now, you may be saying, David, I just heard
something about Clarence Thomas a week and

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:33.330
a half ago before you left.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:39.170
You told us about Clarence Thomas's financial
relationship with a billionaire.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.890
Vacations, yachts, all sorts of different
things.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:43.670
And yes, that is indeed true.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:48.239
But there are now multiple new allegations
against Clarence Thomas.

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:50.150
So let's jump into the first one.

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:56.109
This is from a Washington Post investigation
which says Clarence Thomas has for years claimed

00:00:56.109 --> 00:00:59.160
income from a defunct real estate firm.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.729
The misstatements, which began when a family
business transferred its holdings to another

00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:10.230
company, are part of a pattern that has raised
questions about how the Supreme Court justice

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:16.490
views his obligation to accurately report
details about his finances to the public.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:19.820
The details are actually relatively straightforward
here.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:24.119
Over the last two decades, Clarence Thomas
has reported on financial disclosure forms

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:29.299
that his family was receiving rental income
to the tune of hundreds of thousands of dollars

00:01:29.299 --> 00:01:33.420
from a company called Ginger Limited Partnership.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:40.020
That company, which is a Nebraska real estate
firm launched in the 1980s, has not existed

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.030
since 2006.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:46.649
Okay, That's 17 years that this company has
not existed.

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:50.720
That company was shut down and a separate
firm was created.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.729
The similarly named firm assumed control of
the shuttered company.

00:01:56.729 --> 00:02:02.740
But Clarence Thomas keeps reporting that he's
making between 50 and $100,000 a year from

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:03.869
that company.

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:09.580
There is no mention of the actual company
that exists Ginger Holdings LLC.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:13.500
Now, could this just be an innocent paperwork
error?

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.849
Well, the article addresses that.

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:22.021
It says the previously unreported misstatement
might be dismissed as a paperwork error, but

00:02:22.021 --> 00:02:26.390
it is among a series of errors and omissions
that Thomas has made on these forms over the

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:27.990
last many decades.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:32.330
And if you take them all together, there are
a number of red flags here.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:39.280
Now, this is not the only new controversy
involving Clarence Thomas.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:46.810
The Grio reports Thomas sold real estate to
a donor and didn't report that he did that.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:48.860
This is this is yet another one of these.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:57.840
Oh, it's a mistake, which just by coincidence,
makes it seem as though Thomas has fewer financial

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.830
entanglements with donors and influence peddlers.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:08.549
The Grio reports the 2014 real estate deal
shines a new light on Thomas's decades old

00:03:08.549 --> 00:03:11.390
relationship with Harlan Crowe.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:18.400
That is the real estate magnate and financier
for conservative causes, who I told you about

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.720
a week and a half ago.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:25.391
And indeed, Harlan Crowe bought properties,
three properties belonging to Clarence Thomas

00:03:25.391 --> 00:03:32.870
and his family in a transaction worth more
than $100,000 that Thomas did not ever report.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:37.290
So listen, Clarence Thomas should resign.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:43.290
The totality of the financial picture here
is an absolute nightmare.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:48.760
Over the weekend, I read about something called
the Abe Fortas President Fortas is spelled

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.060
f0rtas.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:58.670
Abe Fortas was a Supreme Court justice appointed
by LBJ in 1965.

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:03.740
He was nominated by LBJ to Chief Justice in
68.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:09.400
But that nomination was eventually withdrawn
because of controversy related to his financial

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.400
dealings.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:17.479
And the Fortas president precedent refers
to this kind of tradition that has emerged

00:04:17.479 --> 00:04:22.360
since his nomination was withdrawn, which
suggests Supreme Court nominees who have been

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:27.960
the subject of significant controversy or
opposition during their confirmation process

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.990
may not be successful in their bid for appointment
to the court.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:39.259
So, in other words, the idea is if a Supreme
Court nominee becomes too controversial, they

00:04:39.259 --> 00:04:41.740
ultimately are not going to succeed.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.350
And it's been cited a whole bunch of different
times.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.110
It came up with Brett Kavanaugh.

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:49.620
We know Brett Kavanaugh ultimately did become
Supreme Court justice, etc..

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.870
And the idea here is it's time to go.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:53.920
It's time to get out.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.949
Now, whether this actually is going to come
to force when it comes to Clarence Thomas

00:04:58.949 --> 00:04:59.949
is on.

00:04:59.949 --> 00:05:00.949
Clear.

00:05:00.949 --> 00:05:05.889
But the argument that's being made is this
all actually goes back to Clarence Thomas.

00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:07.240
Start on the court.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:13.020
It goes back to those hearings where Clarence
Thomas was accused of sexual harassment.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.740
And we should have known at the time that
he was unfit to serve on the court.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:22.300
That, in a way, telegraphed or predicted what
ultimately is coming to pass with Clarence

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:29.630
Thomas, which is a sort of 20 year record
of endless forms of financial impropriety,

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:34.330
questions which ultimately are going to force
him off the court.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:38.630
Now, do I believe Clarence Thomas is actually
going to be forced off of the court?

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:40.240
I really don't.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:41.240
I really don't.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:47.180
And one of the things that we have seen is
that while on paper, there are all sorts of

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:54.420
different rules and conventions and stipulations
and laws when they are actually challenged,

00:05:54.420 --> 00:06:00.889
just because a check or balance is there in
writing does not mean it will actually be

00:06:00.889 --> 00:06:01.889
enforced.

00:06:01.889 --> 00:06:07.980
We've seen so many Hatch Act violations during
the Trump administration, Kellyanne Conway

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:08.980
and others.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:10.990
It all went nowhere.

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:16.889
We've seen all sorts of violations of the
way presidents are supposed to comport themselves

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:18.530
during the Trump administration.

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:22.670
And at least for the most part, there have
been no consequences.

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:26.030
So do I believe that Thomas should resign?

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:27.550
Of course.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:33.530
Do I believe that Clarence Thomas has violated
those judicial canons which say that judges

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:40.110
and justices should avoid not only actual
impropriety, but even the appearance of impropriety?

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.669
Of course, that's been violated by Clarence
Thomas.

00:06:42.669 --> 00:06:45.199
But do I believe he will actually be held
accountable for it?

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:46.460
I don't.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:51.970
If you do, I want to hear from you, because
if anything, we've learned over the last six

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:58.680
years that unless we really have a system
not only willing to have checks and balances,

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:04.130
but to actually enforce them and hold people
accountable for them, nothing happens.

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:06.300
And that's been the greatest lesson of the
last six years for me.

